# Stripe Buy Button Payment Flow (PDF + Application Generator Case Study)

This README documents the existing Stripe Buy Button flow in this repo (PDF purchase) and shows how to adapt it to sell **application generator access** as a case study. It is written so you can hand it to Codex to generate the full, end‑to‑end payment path.

## What you get
- An end‑to‑end flow: UI → Stripe Checkout → redirect → backend → Sanity
- Concrete code examples from the current PDF purchase flow
- A case study for selling access to an application generator
- Security guidance for reliable, auditable entitlements in Sanity

---

## Current PDF purchase flow (what already exists)

### 1) Sanity data model
PDF products live in Sanity, and user entitlements are stored on the user profile.

```ts
// studio/schemaTypes/index.ts (excerpt)
const planPdf = defineType({
  name: "planPdf",
  title: "Plan PDF",
  type: "document",
  fields: [
    { name: "title", type: "string" },
    { name: "format", type: "string" },
    { name: "description", type: "string" },
    { name: "buyButtonId", type: "string" },
    { name: "buyButtonProductId", type: "string" },
    { name: "file", type: "file" },
    { name: "previewImage", type: "image" },
  ],
});

const userProfile = defineType({
  name: "userProfile",
  title: "User Profile",
  type: "document",
  fields: [
    { name: "userId", type: "string" },
    { name: "email", type: "string" },
    { name: "firebaseUid", type: "string" },
    {
      name: "purchasedPdfs",
      type: "array",
      of: [{ type: "reference", to: [{ type: "planPdf" }] }],
    },
  ],
});
```

The important fields:
- `planPdf.buyButtonId` → Stripe Buy Button ID (used by the frontend)
- `planPdf.buyButtonProductId` → Stripe Product ID (used by the webhook)
- `userProfile.purchasedPdfs` → entitlement list

### 2) UI: open buy button + remember pending purchase
When a user clicks Buy, the UI stores the pending PDF ID locally and opens a Stripe buy button modal.

```tsx
// src/App.tsx (excerpt)
const handleStartPdfPurchase = (pdf: PlanPdf) => {
  if (!pdf.id) return;
  localStorage.setItem("pending_plan_pdf_id", pdf.id);
  setPendingPurchasePdf(pdf);
  if (document.referrer) {
    localStorage.setItem("stripe_referrer", document.referrer);
  }
  if (!isAuthed) {
    handleSignIn();
    return;
  }
  setStripeModalOpen(true);
};
```

The Stripe buy button uses the Sanity `buyButtonId` and passes identity fields to Stripe.

```tsx
// src/App.tsx (excerpt)
<stripe-buy-button
  client-reference-id={user?.uid}
  customer-email={user?.email || undefined}
  buy-button-id={pendingPurchasePdf.buyButtonId || "buy_btn_..."}
  publishable-key="pk_live_..."
/>
```

### 3) UI: after checkout redirect, tell backend which PDF was purchased
Stripe redirects back with `session_id`. The UI uses it to call `/purchase` with the pending PDF ID.

```tsx
// src/App.tsx (excerpt)
useEffect(() => {
  const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
  const sessionId = params.get("session_id");
  if (!sessionId || !isAuthed || processedStripeSession === sessionId) return;
  const pendingPdfId = localStorage.getItem("pending_plan_pdf_id");
  if (!pendingPdfId) return;
  const origin = localStorage.getItem("stripe_referrer") || undefined;

  api
    .purchasePlanPdf({ sessionId, pdfId: pendingPdfId, origin })
    .finally(() => {
      setStripeModalOpen(false);
      setPendingPurchasePdf(null);
      setProcessedStripeSession(sessionId);
      localStorage.removeItem("pending_plan_pdf_id");
      params.delete("session_id");
      window.history.replaceState({}, "", `${window.location.pathname}${window.location.search}`);
    });
}, [api, isAuthed, processedStripeSession]);
```

