## Case study: sell access to an Application Generator

The goal: replace “PDF purchase” with “application generator access”. The mechanics stay the same:
- **Stripe Buy Button** still handles checkout
- **Sanity** stores products and entitlements
- **Webhook** is authoritative

### A) New Sanity schema for the generator product
Add a new doc type for the product and store entitlements on the user profile.

```ts
// studio/schemaTypes/index.ts (new types)
const appGeneratorProduct = defineType({
  name: "appGeneratorProduct",
  title: "App Generator Product",
  type: "document",
  fields: [
    { name: "title", type: "string" },
    { name: "description", type: "string" },
    { name: "accessTier", type: "string" },
    { name: "buyButtonId", type: "string" },
    { name: "buyButtonProductId", type: "string" },
  ],
});

// Extend userProfile
const userProfile = defineType({
  name: "userProfile",
  title: "User Profile",
  type: "document",
  fields: [
    // ...existing fields
    {
      name: "appGeneratorAccess",
      title: "App Generator Access",
      type: "array",
      of: [{ type: "reference", to: [{ type: "appGeneratorProduct" }] }],
    },
  ],
});
```

### B) UI: open buy button for generator product
This mirrors PDF purchase. Use `buyButtonId` from Sanity and set `client-reference-id` + `customer-email`.

```tsx
const handleStartGeneratorPurchase = (product: AppGeneratorProduct) => {
  if (!product.id) return;
  localStorage.setItem("pending_app_generator_id", product.id);
  setPendingGenerator(product);
  if (!isAuthed) {
    handleSignIn();
    return;
  }
  setStripeModalOpen(true);
};

<stripe-buy-button
  client-reference-id={user?.uid}
  customer-email={user?.email || undefined}
  buy-button-id={pendingGenerator.buyButtonId}
  publishable-key={STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY}
/>
```

### C) Client redirect handler
When Stripe redirects back with `session_id`, call a backend endpoint that records the purchase (or use it only for UX). Keep the webhook as the source of truth.

```ts
api.purchaseAppGenerator({ sessionId, appGeneratorId: pendingId, origin });
```

### D) Backend: webhook‑first entitlement write
This is the secure, authoritative step. Use the Stripe session to look up the Sanity product by `buyButtonProductId` and write to `userProfile.appGeneratorAccess`.

```ts
app.post("/payments/appGeneratorCheckoutCompleted", async (req, res) => {
  const sig = req.get("stripe-signature");
  const rawBody = (req as { rawBody?: Buffer }).rawBody;
  if (!sig || !rawBody) return res.status(400).send("Webhook Error");

  const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(rawBody, sig, process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET);
  if (event.type === "checkout.session.completed") {
    const session = event.data.object as Stripe.Checkout.Session;
    const metadata = session.metadata || {};
    const productId = metadata.productId || metadata.appGeneratorProductId;

    const productDoc = await sanityClient.fetch(
      `*[_type == "appGeneratorProduct" && buyButtonProductId == $productId][0]{ _id }`,
      { productId }
    );

    // Create or patch userProfile.appGeneratorAccess
    // (idempotent: check alreadyPurchased)
    // ...
  }

  return res.status(200).json({ received: true });
});
```

### E) Gate the application generator UI
Use the user profile entitlements to unlock generator access.

```tsx
const hasGeneratorAccess = (userProfile?.appGeneratorAccess || []).some(p => p.id === generatorProductId);
if (!hasGeneratorAccess) {
  return <CallToActionToPurchase />;
}
```

